WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.868
-I'm so very happy
you came back, Jack.

00:00:03.868 --> 00:00:06.033
Jack Antonoff, thank you
for being here, buddy.

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:07.601
You look great.

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:09.734
I'm excited to get
into this interview

00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:11.434
and discuss things with you.

00:00:11.434 --> 00:00:14.634
You've had an amazing,
busy year yet again.

00:00:14.634 --> 00:00:15.868
-You brought a cheesesteak

00:00:15.868 --> 00:00:17.667
backstage that you said
was from the Roots.

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:19.000
-No, I didn't say it was
from the Roots.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.767
-Is that true?
-It was from me, yes.

00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:22.567
-Okay, just we'll talk.

00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:24.534
But Jimmy walks up with the
cheesesteak, and it was weird.

00:00:24.534 --> 00:00:25.968
And he was like,
"This is from Tariq."

00:00:25.968 --> 00:00:28.067
And I was like...
-What did you think?

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:30.534
-I thought it was really good,
but I thought...

00:00:30.534 --> 00:00:32.234
-It was a bit.
-I didn't plan

00:00:32.234 --> 00:00:34.167
to come out and say it, but then
I saw his face and I was like,

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:35.701
check in about the cheesesteak.

00:00:35.701 --> 00:00:37.501
-But I don't do bits with you.
When I see you,

00:00:37.501 --> 00:00:38.734
I'm all business.

00:00:38.734 --> 00:00:41.934
-That's true.
But I've been busy, Jimmy.

00:00:41.934 --> 00:00:42.934
-Thank you.

00:00:42.934 --> 00:00:44.601
I appreciate
you resetting me up.

00:00:44.601 --> 00:00:45.667
No, let's talk about this

00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:47.067
because you've been
very, very busy.

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:48.868
I don't know how you have time
to do all this stuff.

00:00:48.868 --> 00:00:51.200
First of all, you won a Grammy
for Producer of the Year

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.801
for a second year in a row.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:52.801 --> 00:00:56.734
I mean, that was awesome.
Come on now.

00:00:56.734 --> 00:00:59.601
That alone, you go, wow,
that's a great year.

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:01.934
But you produced three of
the biggest albums of the year.

00:01:01.934 --> 00:01:03.400
Lana Del Rey's "Did You Know

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.133
That There's a Tunnel
Under Ocean Boulevard."

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:07.067
That's Jack.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:10.133
1975's "Being Funny
in a Foreign Language."

00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:12.801
That was you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:16.067
Taylor Swift's "Midnights,"
that was Jack Antonoff.

00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:18.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:18.934 --> 00:01:23.000
By the way, "Cruel Summer."
Can we even talk about this?

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.968
-It's so cool.
-You produced this song.

00:01:24.968 --> 00:01:26.868
You co-wrote it
with Taylor Swift,

00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:28.901
and now,
that was four years ago?

00:01:28.901 --> 00:01:30.634
-Yeah.
-And now it's -- right now,

00:01:30.634 --> 00:01:33.667
today, it's the number-one song
for the second week in a row.

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.133
-It's -- I'm so --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:37.133
Thanks.
-What does it feel like?

00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:42.434
-It feels like I'm loving where
the music business has gone

00:01:42.434 --> 00:01:47.033
because it just melted down into
nothing but what people like.

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:50.434
And you could talk your crap
about this or that,

00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:55.501
but just the fans are God, what
they say goes and all those --

00:01:55.501 --> 00:01:57.400
you know, when I was younger
and you'd make a record

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.367
and you'd say, that's the song

00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:01.701
that if you cracked me open
is my soul, right?

00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:03.734
-Yes.
-But then you're like, this song

00:02:03.734 --> 00:02:06.300
is cool, but that's the song,
and then everyone is saying, no,

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.868
this song that you feel fine
about is the one.

00:02:08.868 --> 00:02:10.367
And then they tell you,
you've got to get behind

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:11.934
that one
and then you'll get to --

00:02:11.934 --> 00:02:14.067
there's just all this --
choosing of singles

00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:16.400
is such a -- because
an album is a patchwork,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.601
and I always see it as like
a house with a front door.

00:02:19.601 --> 00:02:20.868
And, you know, sometimes

00:02:20.868 --> 00:02:22.434
you don't want your front door
painted yellow,

00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:23.767
sometimes you want
it painted black,

00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:25.200
sometimes you want
it painted red.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.667
So the choosing, you guys have
probably been through this,

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:31.467
where someone tells you like,
this is the one and you're like,

00:02:31.467 --> 00:02:34.033
I want to feed my family
and I want to --

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:35.667
But also like --

00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:38.367
but the new world is just sort
of -- it's over. It's cooked.

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:41.267
You know, the idea of a single
is just what's the song that

00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:43.901
if you could get your friends
in a room, you'd play?

00:02:43.901 --> 00:02:46.000
And what happened with "Cruel
Summer" is a testament to that.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.567
It was always our favorite song
in the album.

00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:51.934
And then with nothing, with
no gas on the fire,

00:02:51.934 --> 00:02:54.634
with no one on the business side

00:02:54.634 --> 00:02:56.234
doing anything,
just kids started

00:02:56.234 --> 00:02:57.767
playing it more
and more and more.

00:02:57.767 --> 00:02:59.367
It happened with that song.

00:02:59.367 --> 00:03:00.868
It's happened
with a bunch of things.

00:03:00.868 --> 00:03:03.634
And I just think the music
business

00:03:03.634 --> 00:03:05.767
is in a fun place
for just the work.

00:03:05.767 --> 00:03:07.434
-I also want to say
congratulations.

00:03:07.434 --> 00:03:09.434
You got married recently to...

00:03:09.434 --> 00:03:13.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:15.734
...Margaret Qualley,
who we love.

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:20.300
Yeah.
How was the wedding?

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.133
-I feel like I keep
thinking this,

00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:24.300
which I was like,
I had this idea,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.200
like, I perform,
so I don't care.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.300
I don't care. I just want to be
married to her.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.234
I don't give a [bleep].
Are we not supposed to curse?

00:03:32.234 --> 00:03:34.534
-No, you can do it.
We'll bleep all this out anyway.

00:03:34.534 --> 00:03:35.767
-So in my head I'm like --
-We're going to go right

00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:36.968
from cheesesteaks to this.

00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:38.968
-Yeah, it's like
I don't [bleep] care.

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:41.801
I want to eat a cheesesteak.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:43.334
-What's wrong with you?
-I don't know.

00:03:43.334 --> 00:03:44.334
The S-word became --

00:03:44.334 --> 00:03:45.501
-And I want to [bleep] marry
her.

00:03:45.501 --> 00:03:49.133
-No! Hey! Come on!
-And then...

00:03:49.133 --> 00:03:51.300
[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.067
-Unbelievable. Unbelievable.

00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:54.834
-Can we just do away with the
no cursing on TV?

00:03:54.834 --> 00:03:57.133
-No, we have to have rules,
Jack.

00:03:57.133 --> 00:04:00.434
-Okay, fair. So I didn't care.

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:02.534
And I had this idea,
like, I perform,

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:04.100
so I'm not one of these people,
I don't care.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:07.400
I can do anything, I'm not --
I don't mind being here.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.033
I don't mind being on stage.
It doesn't faze me too much.

00:04:11.033 --> 00:04:15.634
And then as soon as I walked
up there, it was the ultimate...

00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:18.234
It was the most alone
I've ever been in my life,

00:04:18.234 --> 00:04:23.667
in a way, because my bride
hadn't come out yet,

00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:26.100
and I'm very into,
like, making bits.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.367
Even the way I came out here
is a good example, right?

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:31.000
Like Jimmy asked a question and
I kind of like -- kind of

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.901
like alpha'd it a little bit
and turned it

00:04:32.901 --> 00:04:34.300
and did
the cheesesteak thing, right?

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.167
It was a little like -- I'm just
pushing the attention away.

00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:37.868
-What's real in your world,
Jack?

00:04:37.868 --> 00:04:39.200
-And so I'm always pushing away.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.634
And it was the first time
I was just sort of frozen,

00:04:41.634 --> 00:04:44.133
and it felt like
30 years of cynicism

00:04:44.133 --> 00:04:48.868
was exiting my body and...
[ Laughter ]

00:04:48.868 --> 00:04:51.868
-Oh, my gosh.
And you're standing there

00:04:51.868 --> 00:04:53.901
and everyone's looking at you
and this is it, bud.

00:04:53.901 --> 00:04:55.300
-Yeah, it was --
-Let's go.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:56.734
I need my missing --
my other half.

00:04:56.734 --> 00:04:58.300
-But it was beautiful
and it was every cliché

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:01.200
and I kind of melted
and broke in a very sweet way.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.334
And what I...

00:05:05.334 --> 00:05:07.167
haven't felt that way
since the cheesesteak really.

00:05:07.167 --> 00:05:09.167
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:09.167 --> 00:05:11.267
But it's a big deal.

00:05:11.267 --> 00:05:12.701
And I know you guys are both --
-It's a huge deal.

00:05:12.701 --> 00:05:15.133
And I also like --
Is this really fun?

00:05:15.133 --> 00:05:16.734
-I saw your mom in the hallway,

00:05:16.734 --> 00:05:18.734
and I said to her last time --
-What'd you say to her?

00:05:18.734 --> 00:05:20.634
I said,
"What? Are we back at the --"

00:05:20.634 --> 00:05:22.534
I said, "What? Are we back
at the concert?"

00:05:22.534 --> 00:05:24.434
-Yeah.
-Because the last time
we saw each other,

00:05:24.434 --> 00:05:27.267
we were watching you perform
at Radio City Music Hall,

00:05:27.267 --> 00:05:29.501
and you were fantastic.
-You came in hot.

00:05:29.501 --> 00:05:30.968
-I did come in hot.
-Because you were --

00:05:30.968 --> 00:05:33.968
I think it -- Is Radio City
right there?

00:05:33.968 --> 00:05:35.200
-Oh, I came in hot to that show.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.334
-You came in hot
because I think you had,

00:05:36.334 --> 00:05:37.567
like, finished the show.
And then all of a sudden

00:05:37.567 --> 00:05:38.734
we were about to go on stage,
and you just --

00:05:38.734 --> 00:05:41.067
-Did I surprise you?
-It wasn't a surprise,

00:05:41.067 --> 00:05:42.601
but you just sort of blew
through the doors and --

00:05:42.601 --> 00:05:45.534
-Oh, you know what?
-...and launched into a pep talk

00:05:45.534 --> 00:05:47.067
that really --
because it was COVID,

00:05:47.067 --> 00:05:48.601
So we were a little bit like --

00:05:48.601 --> 00:05:50.434
-I was in the wrong place.
Can I tell you?

00:05:50.434 --> 00:05:52.534
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.534 --> 00:05:54.501
I thought I was going to go
right to my seats.

00:05:54.501 --> 00:05:56.200
I didn't want to go back
and say hi to you.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.567
-Of course you come back
and say hi.

00:05:57.567 --> 00:05:59.367
-I would say back and say
hi to you after the show,

00:05:59.367 --> 00:06:00.934
not before the show.
-No, we like that.

00:06:00.934 --> 00:06:02.901
-No, you have a thing.
You have a job to do.

00:06:02.901 --> 00:06:05.501
I don't want to -- I go --
I ended up in the wrong place,

00:06:05.501 --> 00:06:07.501
and all of a sudden
I'm with the band and I'm going

00:06:07.501 --> 00:06:09.968
"Let's go, let's huddle up,
let's get in here, guys.

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:11.968
We got to go out there
and crush it tonight."

00:06:11.968 --> 00:06:15.667
-It was a funny scene because it
was you, my parents.

00:06:15.667 --> 00:06:17.033
-Yes.
-Springsteen.

00:06:17.033 --> 00:06:19.100
-Bruce Springsteen.
-And the band.

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:20.834
And then we went and played.

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:22.334
But it was
a weird crew backstage.

00:06:22.334 --> 00:06:24.634
-It's weird to give a pep
talk around Bruce Springsteen

00:06:24.634 --> 00:06:27.400
because I think he would be
the most peppiest talker.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.400
Like, he'd be like,
"You got to get out there!

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.834
1, 2, 3!"
[ Grunting ]

00:06:32.834 --> 00:06:34.100
[ as Springsteen ]
You know, the first time,

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.934
Jack, the first time
you play Radio City,

00:06:36.934 --> 00:06:41.334
you know, you come out and
the curtains close, you know.

00:06:41.334 --> 00:06:43.968
But you can you
can hear the lights

00:06:43.968 --> 00:06:45.901
and you can hear the crowd.
-Should I be you?

00:06:45.901 --> 00:06:47.300
-And you can --
[ Normal voice ] Yeah.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:48.534
-[ Screaming ]

00:06:48.534 --> 00:06:52.734
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:52.734 --> 00:06:54.167
-Totally ruined the vibe.

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:56.067
-No, no.
-Ruined the vibe.

00:06:56.067 --> 00:06:59.133
Hi, guys. Hi, Jack. Hey, man.

00:06:59.133 --> 00:07:01.634
Hey, Bruce.
I'm a big fan, man.

00:07:01.634 --> 00:07:03.367
-I love Bruce Springsteen
more than anything,

00:07:03.367 --> 00:07:05.701
but in life, you can be a Jimmy
or a Bruce, and I'm a Jimmy.

00:07:05.701 --> 00:07:07.934
[ Audience awws ]

00:07:07.934 --> 00:07:09.667
-But you want to hang
with the Bruce.

00:07:09.667 --> 00:07:12.267
[ Laughter ]

00:07:12.267 --> 00:07:13.400
-Yeah, but you hang
with the Roots.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.033
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:16.033 --> 00:07:18.067
I was trying to pitch you
some songs backstage

00:07:18.067 --> 00:07:20.901
because I always want you
to be getting number ones,

00:07:20.901 --> 00:07:22.434
and that's all I care about.

00:07:22.434 --> 00:07:24.067
And that's why every time
I see you, I'm all business.

00:07:24.067 --> 00:07:26.100
I want to give you
what I'm thinking

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:27.367
and give you
the number-one song.

00:07:27.367 --> 00:07:29.968
-What have you got today?

00:07:29.968 --> 00:07:31.667
-I got one song.
-Okay.

00:07:31.667 --> 00:07:34.167
-It's called "Mexico City."

00:07:34.167 --> 00:07:35.968
-Do the Roots know it?
-No, I made it up.

00:07:35.968 --> 00:07:38.000
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:44.434
-♪ How are things
in Mexico City? ♪

00:07:44.434 --> 00:07:47.334
♪♪

00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:48.901
That's it. That's all I got.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:51.667
You take that and you...

00:07:51.667 --> 00:07:53.634
You make, like,
a snowball out of it.

00:07:53.634 --> 00:07:55.934
Or it could be a snowball --
No, you don't want to use it

00:07:55.934 --> 00:07:57.367
for what, throw things? No.

00:07:57.367 --> 00:07:59.467
Jackie,
you carve it with a little --

00:07:59.467 --> 00:08:01.934
with a little chisel
and your little hammer

00:08:01.934 --> 00:08:03.133
and you carve
that little snowball

00:08:03.133 --> 00:08:05.734
into a beautiful ice figurine

00:08:05.734 --> 00:08:09.033
of a little German --

00:08:09.033 --> 00:08:12.734
No. A little German boy
putting a -- a Dutch boy.

00:08:12.734 --> 00:08:14.534
-Putting what?
-A little Dutch boy

00:08:14.534 --> 00:08:16.234
putting his finger in a dike...

00:08:16.234 --> 00:08:18.601
[ Laughter ]

00:08:18.601 --> 00:08:20.400
To stop the leak.

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:25.868
like Hans Christian Andersen
himself.

00:08:25.868 --> 00:08:29.000
-Who on this program has
the balls to air that?

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.300
[ Laughter ]
[ Rimshot ]

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.901
-What are you talking about it?
We don't cut anything here.

00:08:33.901 --> 00:08:35.667
-Have some very guts.
-Yeah. Come on.

00:08:35.667 --> 00:08:38.167
-Absolute guts because --
-He's stopping a leak

00:08:38.167 --> 00:08:39.501
from a town being destroyed.

00:08:39.501 --> 00:08:41.434
-That's nice.
I'll tell you something.

00:08:41.434 --> 00:08:45.133
I -- I -- What I liked about
how the song --

00:08:45.133 --> 00:08:46.267
Is it called "Mexico City"?

00:08:46.267 --> 00:08:48.868
-Yeah, it's a working title.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:48.868 --> 00:08:51.033
-What I like about it
is that --

00:08:51.033 --> 00:08:52.701
and I'm not even doing a bit --

00:08:52.701 --> 00:08:54.934
I heard it and I was like, "Ha."

00:08:54.934 --> 00:08:57.200
And then I was like,
how are things in Mexico City?

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:58.334
-Right?
-Well, if you were like,

00:08:58.334 --> 00:09:00.267
"I'm in love right now,"
I'd be like, "Okay."

00:09:00.267 --> 00:09:01.901
You know,
like, but how are things?

00:09:01.901 --> 00:09:06.501
-This could be about
maybe his loved one left him.

00:09:06.501 --> 00:09:08.534
-Yeah.
-And restarted her life

00:09:08.534 --> 00:09:10.434
in Mexico City.
-I would go --

00:09:10.434 --> 00:09:12.400
I would go with,
how are things in Mexico City?

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:14.033
I wouldn't go with
the loved one there.

00:09:14.033 --> 00:09:15.467
Maybe the character is broken.

00:09:15.467 --> 00:09:17.067
The loved one leaves him
in New York,

00:09:17.067 --> 00:09:18.934
and then he's fantasizing
about Mexico City

00:09:18.934 --> 00:09:21.033
just like as a place to go.
-Oh!

00:09:21.033 --> 00:09:24.067
[ Applause ]

00:09:24.067 --> 00:09:25.934
This is what I'm talking about.
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:25.934 --> 00:09:30.534
-No!
-♪ How are things
in Mexico City? ♪

00:09:30.534 --> 00:09:34.334
♪♪

00:09:34.334 --> 00:09:37.868
-Because life around here
has gotten real weird lately.

00:09:37.868 --> 00:09:39.133
Like lately as a soft --

00:09:39.133 --> 00:09:42.400
-♪ Latelyyyy! ♪

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:44.033
-Yeah.

00:09:44.033 --> 00:09:49.234
And then you see, Jenny left.
-Ooh.

00:09:49.234 --> 00:09:51.467
-Yeah. What have you got left?
-Daddy's a [bleep]

00:09:51.467 --> 00:09:53.701
-Yeah.
Mommy never been there for me.

00:09:53.701 --> 00:09:55.067
-Mommy has never been there.

00:09:55.067 --> 00:09:57.133
-Yeah. My job sucks.
-What gives, what gives,

00:09:57.133 --> 00:09:59.000
what gives?
-What gives, dude?

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:00.868
-Yeah.
-Oh.

00:10:00.868 --> 00:10:03.167
-But I saw a postcard.
-Yeah. On Expedia.

00:10:03.167 --> 00:10:07.033
-But then I flip it
that had Guadalajara on it

00:10:07.033 --> 00:10:08.534
just so it could be
that part of the song

00:10:08.534 --> 00:10:09.667
they're like, "Wait, I thought
we were talking Mexico City."

00:10:09.667 --> 00:10:10.601
-Nope.
-Yeah.

00:10:10.601 --> 00:10:11.868
-Went to Guadalajara instead.

00:10:11.868 --> 00:10:14.167
-And I thought a guy like me?

00:10:14.167 --> 00:10:15.267
Question mark.
-Yeah.

00:10:15.267 --> 00:10:17.033
-Could it be? Question mark.

00:10:17.033 --> 00:10:20.400
-And I got a ticket
and it's one way.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:21.734
-Where?

00:10:21.734 --> 00:10:22.734
-♪ I say ♪

00:10:22.734 --> 00:10:24.300
♪♪

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:25.467
No, no, I can't do it again.

00:10:25.467 --> 00:10:27.434
I can't do it again.
I can't do it anyway.

00:10:27.434 --> 00:10:29.834
Just those little nuggets
I'll keep giving it to you.

00:10:29.834 --> 00:10:33.767
And just when they're
number ones, you let me know.

00:10:33.767 --> 00:10:35.501
-Oh, the [bleep] lawyer
will let you know.

00:10:35.501 --> 00:10:37.334
[ Laughter ]

00:10:37.334 --> 00:10:38.834
You wrote it.
-I just want to make sure --

00:10:38.834 --> 00:10:40.567
-You think I'm going to steal
your lyric and melody

00:10:40.567 --> 00:10:42.033
while you just performed it?

00:10:42.033 --> 00:10:43.467
-No, I'm giving it to you.

00:10:43.467 --> 00:10:46.934
This is a verbal contract.
It's yours free of charge.

00:10:46.934 --> 00:10:49.133
"Mexico City" is your next...
-Okay.

00:10:49.133 --> 00:10:51.834
[ Laughter ]

00:10:51.834 --> 00:10:53.000
No, because I don't --

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.267
-It's dumb enough that
it could catch.

00:10:55.267 --> 00:10:57.267
Like right now,
like part of me...

00:10:57.267 --> 00:11:01.067
♪ How are things
in Mexico City? ♪

00:11:01.067 --> 00:11:02.634
♪♪

00:11:02.634 --> 00:11:03.667
Do you remember it?

00:11:03.667 --> 00:11:05.300
♪♪

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:09.934
♪ Because it round here
has been real weird lately ♪

00:11:09.934 --> 00:11:11.033
Ringo fill.

00:11:11.033 --> 00:11:13.234
[ Drum solo ]

00:11:13.234 --> 00:11:14.901
♪ My dad ain't there ♪

00:11:14.901 --> 00:11:22.367
[ Both singing indistinctly ]

00:11:22.367 --> 00:11:25.901
♪ How are things
in Mexico City? ♪

00:11:25.901 --> 00:11:30.067
♪♪

00:11:30.067 --> 00:11:31.200
-Alright, let's think about it.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:34.601 --> 00:11:37.400
You're doing a real song.
You're doing a real --

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:39.300
You're here.
Bleachers are performing

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:42.434
"Modern Girl," by the way.
It's a fantastic song.

00:11:42.434 --> 00:11:44.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:44.133 --> 00:11:46.934
And you'll be performing for us
this evening.

00:11:46.934 --> 00:11:49.167
-The first time
we've ever performed it live.

00:11:49.167 --> 00:11:51.133
And I love that
because it feels like --

00:11:51.133 --> 00:11:53.467
There's something that feels
like '60s and classic about it.

00:11:53.467 --> 00:11:54.868
Like, we have to,
like, get it together

00:11:54.868 --> 00:11:56.734
and we got to play it on TV
and we got to get it right.

00:11:56.734 --> 00:12:00.467
Like, the nerves are amazing.
And one last thing before we go.

00:12:00.467 --> 00:12:03.133
Last time we were here
was the first show

00:12:03.133 --> 00:12:05.901
we ever played after COVID.

00:12:05.901 --> 00:12:07.634
So this stage has like
a real energy to us.

00:12:07.634 --> 00:12:08.834
It's like we met here

00:12:08.834 --> 00:12:10.667
as a band the first time
to play after COVID,

00:12:10.667 --> 00:12:13.701
now the first time to play live
on the new album. It's special.

00:12:13.701 --> 00:12:15.667
-And I will say I didn't
even get a chance to tell you.

00:12:15.667 --> 00:12:17.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:12:17.801 --> 00:12:19.801
That concert I saw at Radio City

00:12:19.801 --> 00:12:21.601
was one of the best shows
I've ever seen.

00:12:21.601 --> 00:12:24.067
I loved it so much.
You guys were unbelievable.

00:12:24.067 --> 00:12:25.934
If you got a chance to see
Bleachers live, go,

00:12:25.934 --> 00:12:28.701
run, run, run and you will never
forget it. It's fantastic.

00:12:28.701 --> 00:12:29.868
You have great fans.

00:12:29.868 --> 00:12:32.234
-The best.
-Jack Antonoff, everyone.

00:12:32.234 --> 00:12:33.367
That's what I'm talking about.

00:12:33.367 --> 00:12:35.033
"Modern Girl" is out now.

00:12:35.033 --> 00:12:36.834
We'll be right back
with Mo Rocca.

00:12:36.834 --> 00:12:37.968
Stick around, everybody.

